an>
Output: <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>
</code></pre><p>Explanation: The highest possible height setting that yield=
s at least <code>k =3D 7</code> meters of wood is 3, which yields 8 meters =
of wood. Any height setting higher than this will yield less than 7 meters =
of wood.</p><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>It's always possible to attain at least <code>k</code> meters of wood.<=
/li>
<li>There's at least one tree.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>At first, it might strike you as strange that this problem is in the <em=
>Binary Search</em> chapter. The given input array isn't necessarily sorted=
, so how is binary search applicable here? Well, this is an example of a co=
mmon application of binary search, where the search space does not encompas=
s the input array.</p>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s consider a visualization of four trees of heights <code>[2=
, 6, 3, 8]</code> and assume k =3D 7:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
